%N A402357 Number of strongly connected digraphs on n unlabeled nodes such that for every node h, every strongly connected component of D - h receives exactly one edge from outside.
%C A402357 All digraphs have no self-loops. The one-node graph qualifies vacuously. Isomorphisms do not distinguish a root.
%C A402357 The unique-entry-edge condition implies reducibility rooted at every node. It requires exactly one incoming edge per component, not merely one target node for those edges.
%C A402357 The prefix 1, 1, 2, 4, 9, 26 coincides with A002773 (simple matroids), but a(7) = 78 while A002773(7) = 101, so the agreement is coincidence.
%D A402357 F. Harary and E. M. Palmer, Graphical Enumeration, Academic Press, NY, 1973.
%D A402357 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Flow graph reducibility, SIAM J. Comput. 1 (1972), 188-202.
%D A402357 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Characterizations of reducible flow graphs, J. ACM 21 (1974), 367-375.
%D A402357 R. W. Robinson, Counting digraphs with restrictions on the strong components, in T.-H. Ku (ed.), Combinatorics and Graph Theory '95, World Scientific, Singapore, 1995, pp. 343-354.
%D A402357 R. E. Tarjan, Testing flow graph reducibility, J. Comput. System Sci. 9 (1974), 355-365.
%H A402357 Gregory Morse, <a href="/A402357/b402357.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..172</a>
%H A402357 Gregory Morse, <a href="/A397968/a397968.py.txt">Required Python module (unlabeled_reducible_cfgs.py)</a>, Aug 2026.
